+// WEBKIT_INDEPENDENT_SCROLL (see ThreadedScrollingTree.cpp for the main half):
+// let a scroll composite proceed while the compositor is waiting for tiles.
+//
+// isOnlyRenderingUpdatePendingAndWaitingForTiles() below encodes the rule that
+// *only* a RenderingUpdate-reason composite should be held back for tiles -- an
+// AsyncScrolling composite may go ahead and re-composite the committed scene with
+// whatever tiles it already has. resume() (:227) and frameComplete() (:632) both
+// honour that rule, but scheduleUpdateLocked()'s State::Idle branch does not: it
+// tests the raw m_state.isWaitingForTiles, so it parks the composite in
+// State::Scheduled and never starts the render timer, whatever the reason.
+//
+// That is the whole ballgame on a page like franceinfo, which repaints the
+// document every scroll frame: didChangeTiles() only clears isWaitingForTiles
+// once pendingTiles() drains to zero (:260), so under a continuous repaint storm
+// it never clears, and scroll composites are starved while the compositor thread
+// sits ~84% idle (device-measured: main thread 92% pinned, compositor 16%,
+// 6.3 composites/s, and every ftrace freeze classified TILES PAINTING even though
+// the scrolling thread was committing offsets independently, scrollapply thr=S
+// 197 vs thr=M 37).
+//
+// With this on, the Idle branch uses the same reason-aware predicate as its two
+// siblings, so scrolling composites keep flowing against already-committed tiles
+// while the main thread repaints. Lossy in the intended way: those frames show
+// stale/low-res tiles for newly-exposed content instead of freezing.
+static bool independentScrollEnabled()
+{
+    static bool enabled = [] {
+        const char* env = getenv("WEBKIT_INDEPENDENT_SCROLL");
+        return env && *env && strcmp(env, "0");
+    }();
+    return enabled;
+}
